WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.840
Picture a good cook's small habits.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:11.014
You set up a new tray: paper, a little butter, twelve cookies, and that same tray goes into the oven.

3
00:00:11.014 --> 00:00:17.066
You jot in the kitchen notebook that tray two went in at ten past, and the tray goes on, unchanged.

4
00:00:17.066 --> 00:00:21.223
You dip a spoon in the soup, and you get an answer back: needs salt.

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:23.256
Each habit has one clear job.

6
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:26.460
From across the room, you can tell which one it is.

7
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:32.876
Kotlin has five tiny functions for jobs like these: let, run, with, apply and also.

8
00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:36.194
And here's the question many of us are shy to ask.

9
00:00:36.194 --> 00:00:37.721
They all look the same.

10
00:00:37.721 --> 00:00:41.836
Is there a real difference, or do people just pick a favorite?

11
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:44.893
Here's code from the recipe app.

12
00:00:44.893 --> 00:00:51.671
It sets up a program for the oven, 180 degrees, 12 minutes, oat cookies, and writes a line to the log.

13
00:00:51.671 --> 00:00:53.333
Nothing is wrong with it.

14
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:58.516
But it says program dot on every line, and nothing marks where the setup ends.

15
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:01.557
Kotlin code often says it like this.

16
00:01:01.557 --> 00:01:05.494
apply sets up the new object, and hands back the same object.

17
00:01:05.494 --> 00:01:10.464
Then also writes the log line in passing, and lets the value go on unchanged.

18
00:01:10.464 --> 00:01:14.983
Those are the first two habits: set up a tray, and note it in passing.

19
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.203
And here's the catch.

20
00:01:17.203 --> 00:01:20.990
The compiler accepts almost any of the five, almost anywhere.

21
00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.969
Pick the wrong one, and the code still compiles.

22
00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:26.700
It just hands back the wrong thing, quietly.

23
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:28.955
So let's test them.

24
00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:31.321
A recipe for pancakes, 4 servings.

25
00:01:31.321 --> 00:01:35.217
The same block, servings times 2, goes through all five.

26
00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:37.025
What does each line print?

27
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.831
let, run and with print 8.

28
00:01:41.831 --> 00:01:44.576
also and apply print the recipe itself.

29
00:01:44.576 --> 00:01:48.660
Same block, same recipe, and two of them threw the 8 away.

30
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:53.292
It's like asking someone to taste the soup, and getting the whole pot back.

31
00:01:53.292 --> 00:01:57.953
So the point really is: also and apply hand back the value they were called on.

32
00:01:57.953 --> 00:02:00.727
let, run and with hand back the block's result.

33
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:03.051
These aren't keywords.

34
00:02:03.051 --> 00:02:06.466
Each one is a tiny function you could write yourself.

35
00:02:06.466 --> 00:02:08.463
Here are let and apply by hand.

36
00:02:08.463 --> 00:02:13.166
myLet calls the block with the value, and returns what the block returns.

37
00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:16.195
myApply runs the block, then returns the value.

38
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.349
The other difference is the type of the block.

39
00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:24.990
For let, it's a plain function type, so the value arrives as the lambda's parameter, it.

40
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.618
For apply, it's a lambda with a receiver.

41
00:02:27.618 --> 00:02:32.362
Inside it, the value is this, so minutes equals 12 means this dot minutes.

42
00:02:32.362 --> 00:02:37.105
Java has no lambda like that, so the Java helper says p dot on every line.

43
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:42.238
So there are two differences, and two questions pick the function.

44
00:02:42.238 --> 00:02:44.659
First: what should the call hand back?

45
00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:47.716
The same value, to keep chaining it or store it?

46
00:02:47.716 --> 00:02:48.927
Then apply or also.

47
00:02:48.927 --> 00:02:50.965
Something new, computed from it?

48
00:02:50.965 --> 00:02:52.367
Then let, run or with.

49
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.304
Second: how does the block use the value?

50
00:02:55.304 --> 00:03:01.173
If it mostly sets or calls the value's own members, this reads best: apply, run and with.

51
00:03:01.173 --> 00:03:05.591
If it passes the value on to other functions, use it: also and let.

52
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.864
And each cell has its kitchen habit.

53
00:03:08.864 --> 00:03:10.436
apply sets up a new tray.

54
00:03:10.436 --> 00:03:12.009
also notes it in passing.

55
00:03:12.009 --> 00:03:15.216
let tastes with a spoon, and brings back an answer.

56
00:03:15.216 --> 00:03:19.178
run reads the oven's own dials, and tells you the minutes left.

57
00:03:19.178 --> 00:03:22.386
And with reads off the card you're already holding.

58
00:03:22.386 --> 00:03:24.210
run and with do the same job.

59
00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.795
with reads well on its own line, and run fits in a chain.

60
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.615
Now the names.

61
00:03:29.615 --> 00:03:36.085
These five are the scope functions: small inline functions that run a block with one value in hand.

62
00:03:36.085 --> 00:03:38.960
And that value is called the context object.

63
00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:42.375
Here's where the kitchen picture breaks.

64
00:03:42.375 --> 00:03:45.197
A note in the notebook can't change the soup.

65
00:03:45.197 --> 00:03:47.893
But Kotlin doesn't hold a block to its job.

66
00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:49.210
This dish is planned.

67
00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.662
We call also, and inside, we make a copy with the stage set to cooking.

68
00:03:53.662 --> 00:03:55.480
What stage does cooking have?

69
00:03:58.467 --> 00:03:59.035
Planned.

70
00:03:59.035 --> 00:04:03.294
also threw the copy away, and handed back the original dish.

71
00:04:03.294 --> 00:04:06.559
Kotlin 2.4.21 compiles this without a warning.

72
00:04:06.559 --> 00:04:12.024
The name tells the reader what you meant, and the compiler checks none of it.

73
00:04:12.024 --> 00:04:15.503
If you want the copy, use let, or just call copy.

74
00:04:16.433 --> 00:04:18.647
The five share one more trick.

75
00:04:18.647 --> 00:04:25.435
A val can be assigned only once, yet this line assigns one inside a lambda, and it compiles.

76
00:04:25.435 --> 00:04:27.575
The jar gets its label, J-62.

77
00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:27.870
How?

78
00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:35.027
also declares a contract: a promise to the compiler that the block runs right here, exactly once.

79
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:44.443
Write your own also without that contract, and the same line fails: Captured values cannot be initialized because of possible reassignments.

80
00:04:44.443 --> 00:04:46.548
The compiler trusts the promise.

81
00:04:46.548 --> 00:04:47.797
It never checks it.

82
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.324
So before you pick one, ask whether you need one at all.

83
00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:55.561
A plain call, or an if on a val, is often clearer.

84
00:04:55.561 --> 00:05:00.156
A scope function with one line inside is often a plain call in costume.

85
00:05:00.156 --> 00:05:01.450
And don't nest them.

86
00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:05.075
Two its, or two thises, in one place are easy to mix up.

87
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.862
One caution for the next section.

88
00:05:07.862 --> 00:05:13.452
x?.let, then the Elvis operator, then run, looks like an if and an else.

89
00:05:13.452 --> 00:05:15.005
It isn't always one.

90
00:05:15.933 --> 00:05:19.099
So, is there a real difference between the five?

91
00:05:19.099 --> 00:05:20.221
Yes, exactly two.

92
00:05:20.221 --> 00:05:22.925
Is the value this or it inside the block?

93
00:05:22.925 --> 00:05:26.949
And does the call hand back the value, or the block's result?

94
00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:28.532
Two questions, one cell.

95
00:05:29.233 --> 00:05:30.124
Quick check.

96
00:05:30.124 --> 00:05:32.130
menu.add returns a slot id.

97
00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.511
You want to log that id, and keep it flowing on, unchanged.

98
00:05:36.511 --> 00:05:38.071
Which one do you use?

99
00:05:41.067 --> 00:05:41.450
also.

100
00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:47.130
You want the same value back, and the block hands it to the logger, as it.

101
00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:49.279
menu.add(dish).also, log it.
